['Here is an example of C code that accomplishes what you're asking for. This program generates three random integers larger than 100, adds them together and stores the result in both a regular int and long long variable. It then prints all these values. Please note that the randomness of the numbers generated is not truly random. They are pseudo-randomly generated based on a seed value which could be the same across different runs.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <time.h>

int main() {
    srand(time(0)); // Initialize random number generator

    int a = rand() % 899 + 101; // Generate random integer between 101 and 999
    int b = rand() % 899 + 101; // Repeat for second and third integers
    int c = rand() % 899 + 101;

    long long sum2 = (long long)a + b + c; // Add integers as long long to prevent overflow
    int sum = a + b + c; // Add integers normally

    printf("a: %d\n", a);
    printf("b: %d\n", b);
    printf("c: %d\n", c);
    printf("sum: %d\n", sum);
    printf("sum2: %lld\n", sum2); // Use %lld for long long in formatting string

    return 0;
}
```
This code will generate three random integers between 101 and 999, add them together both as regular integers and as long longs to handle possible overflow, print all the numbers and their sums, and then terminate. As mentioned before, the randomness of the generated numbers is not true random but pseudo-random based on a seed value which could be the same across different runs.
']

