['Here's a simple implementation in C that does as you asked. This code uses `malloc` and `calloc` functions from the standard library to dynamically allocate memory, then checks for errors. 

It also includes command line argument parsing using `strtol` function. It converts the string passed via command line into a long integer. Please note that it doesn't perform any error checking on this conversion process - you might want to add some code to handle incorrect inputs or other possible errors.

```C
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 2) {
        printf("Usage: %s size\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    // Convert the command line argument to a long integer.
    char *endptr;
    long val = strtol(argv[1], &endptr, 10);

    // Check for errors during conversion.
    if (*endptr != '\0') {
        printf("Error: Not a valid number.\n");
        return 2;
    }

    // Check for overflows.
    if (val > SIZE_MAX || val < 0) {
        printf("Error: Size is out of range.\n");
        return 3;
    }

    size_t size = (size_t)val;

    // Allocate memory for the buffer.
    char *buffer = malloc(size);

    // Check if allocation was successful.
    if (buffer == NULL) {
        printf("Error: Could not allocate memory.\n");
        return 4;
    }

    // Zero out the allocated memory to prevent undefined behavior from accessing uninitialized data.
    memset(buffer, 0, size);

    // Print the contents of the buffer.
    for (size_t i = 0; i < size; ++i) {
        printf("%d ", buffer[i]);
    }

    printf("\n");

    // Free allocated memory.
    free(buffer);

    return 0;
}
```

This program will allocate a buffer of the specified size, print out its contents as integers (note that it's likely to print out many zeros if you don't initialize the array), and then free the allocated memory. The `SIZE_MAX` is a macro defined in `stdlib.h` which represents the maximum value for the type `size_t`, ensuring that we don't overflow this type when converting the command line argument to a long integer.
']

